    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for clarifying that. so if my LDL is high, what actually happens in the arteries? like, how does it physically cause problems over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When your LDL cholesterol levels are high, LDL particles can accumulate in the walls of your arteries. Over time, these particles can combine with other substances, such as calcium, to form plaque. This plaque buildup narrows the arteries, a condition known as atherosclerosis. As the arteries become narrower, blood flow is restricted, which can lead to various cardiovascular problems, including heart attacks, strokes, and peripheral artery disease. Additionally, the plaque can rupture, causing blood clots that can further obstruct blood flow or break off and travel to other parts of the body, leading to life-threatening complications. It's crucial to manage high LDL levels to prevent these serious health issues.",
      "source": "target",
